    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d40">

                <pb facs="0057" n="47" sameAs="frus:frus1941v06/pg47"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War, 1939/10113: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-29T22:00:00-04:00">April 29, 1941—10
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>250. [Here follows summary of telegram printed <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra</hi>.] The principal basis for such an approach is of course the common
                    bond of race, language and history. While the circumstances are somewhat
                    different in the case of Brazil, you are requested in your discretion to discuss
                    the matter with Aranha<anchor n="4" xml:id="d40-f1"/> or with Nabuco.<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d40-f2"/> It is the feeling of the Department that an
                    expression of interest by the Brazilian Ambassador in Madrid might be very
                    helpful.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d40-f1" n="4" xml:id="d40-f1-content">
                <p>Oswaldo Aranha, Brazilian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d40-f2" n="5" xml:id="d40-f2-content">
                <p>Mauricio Nabnco, Secretary General of the Brazilian Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
